1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Newer models handle reasoning differently than the ones before them,

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:09,000
and the change trips up developers sometimes. Adaptive thinking is the current path,

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,000
effort has moved, and the old budget tokens knob is on the way out.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:19,000
This lesson walks through all three, and why each one is tied to a named model. Let's dig in.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Let's start with three ideas you'll keep running into. First, adaptive thinking,

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,000
the current form of what the docs call extended thinking. It's how you let Claude reason before

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
it answers. You set thinking type to adaptive, you don't hand it a token budget, and Claude decides

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:43,000
when to think and how much. Second, effort. That's a separate dial for how hard the model works on a

9
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:51,000
response, and it lives at output config.effort, not at the top of your request. Third, the legacy

10
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:56,000
budget tokens mode. That's the older approach, and it's mid-deprecation, rejected outright on

11
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:02,000
the newest models, adaptive, effort, and the fading budget tokens. Keep those three straight,

12
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:08,000
and the rest of this lesson clicks into place. Here's the part that surprises people. The default

13
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:16,000
depends on the model, so learn the roster. On Opus 4.8 and Opus 4.7, adaptive is the only thinking

14
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:22,000
mode, and it's off unless you explicitly turn it on. Leave the thinking field out of your request,

15
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:29,000
and those two answer without any extended reasoning at all. To get it, you set thinking type

16
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:34,000
to adaptive, and from there Claude interleaves its thinking between tool calls on its own.

17
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Sonnet 5 flips that default, omit the thinking field there, and it runs adaptive on its own,

18
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:48,000
so if you don't want thinking, you pass thinking type disabled explicitly. And Fable 5 is always on

19
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,000
with no way to switch it off, so the default behavior depends entirely on which model you're

20
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:00,000
calling, which is exactly why you always name the model. Now the effort control, which is separate

21
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:06,000
from turning thinking on. Effort decides how much work the model puts into a response, and the single

22
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:13,000
most common mistake is putting it in the wrong place. It's nested at output.config.effort, not

23
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:20,000
at the root of your request. Key it at the top level, and it simply won't take. The values run

24
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:27,000
from low to medium to high, which is the default, and then up to x high and max. That x high setting

25
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:36,000
is available on Fable 5, Opus 4.8, Opus 4.7, and Sonnet 5. So think of effort as a dial you

26
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:43,000
retune per root, and always reach for it under output.config. Let's put adaptive and the legacy

27
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:49,000
mode side by side, because the contrast is the whole point. Adaptive is thinking type adaptive

28
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:56,000
with no fixed budget. You hand control to Claude, and it sizes its own reasoning for the task.

29
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:01,000
That's the current path on the newest models. The legacy mode is thinking type enabled paired

30
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:07,000
with an explicit budget tokens number. On the current frontier models, that call now returns

31
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:14,000
a 400 error. Same intent, reason before answering, but the old way of expressing it is rejected. If

32
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:20,000
your code still sends budget tokens to a current model, that's a migration bug waiting to surface.

33
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,000
This is why every thinking answer needs a named model, because the same call behaves differently

34
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:34,000
depending on where you send it. Walk the ladder with me. On Fable 5, Sonnet 5, Opus 4.8, and Opus

35
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:43,000
4.7, budget tokens returns a 400 today. On Opus 4.6, it's transitional, still working as a deprecated

36
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:51,000
escape hatch while you migrate. And on Sonnet 4.5 and Opus 4.5, it's not just allowed. It's the only

37
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:58,000
way to enable thinking, since those models have no adaptive mode. So the truth of any thinking

38
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:04,000
question changes model by model. Never state whether budget tokens works without saying which

39
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:11,000
model you mean. Now, a quick word on visibility, because seeing the thinking and being charged for

40
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:20,000
it are separate concerns. On the newest models, Fable 5, Opus 4.8, Opus 4.7, and Sonnet 5, thinking

41
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:26,000
display defaults to omitted. The thinking blocks still stream. They just come through with empty

42
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:33,000
text, so you don't see the reasoning. If you want a readable version, set display to summarized, and

43
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:39,000
Claude surfaces a summary of what it worked through. Here's the key point. Display only changes what's

44
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:45,000
shown to you. It has no effect on cost. The thinking is billed the same either way. So flip

45
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:51,000
it to summarized freely when you want visibility without worrying about your bill. When you're doing

46
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:58,000
multi-turn work or a tool loop, thinking blocks come with a handling rule you have to respect.

47
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:04,000
The thinking blocks in the latest assistant message have to go back to the API exactly as you

48
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:10,000
received them, untouched. That includes redacted thinking, which is encrypted reasoning. Claude

49
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:16,000
returns when some content is filtered. It carries a signature, and it needs the same careful handling.

50
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:22,000
If you edit or strip a thinking block or its signature in that latest turn, your next call

51
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:28,000
returns a 400. Prior turns are more forgiving. You can drop their thinking, but if you keep it,

52
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:33,000
keep it unmodified. Treat these blocks as read-only and pass them straight through.

53
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:40,000
One more constraint kicks in when thinking and tools are on together. Normally you get four

54
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:46,000
tool choice options, but with extended thinking active, only auto and none are allowed. If you

55
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:52,000
try to force a call, either with any or by naming a specific tool, the request errors out. So you

56
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:58,000
can't hard require a tool while thinking is on. Layer that on top of the rule we just covered,

57
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:03,000
where the latest assistant thinking blocks travel back unmodified, and you've got both

58
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:09,000
constraints to design around. Plan your tool loop so it leans on auto, lets Claude decide when to

59
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:16,000
call, and never mutates the thinking it hands you back between steps. Let's recap. Adaptive thinking

60
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:22,000
is the current model-specific reasoning path. On Opus 4.8 and 4.7, it's off until you set it.

61
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:29,000
Effort lives under output config rather than the request root, and legacy budget tokens

62
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:34,000
is fading fast. One thing to watch, always name the model in your code.

63
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:39,000
That covers adaptive thinking and effort control. I'll see you in the next one.

